</section>

<!-- ── §02 Formula ── -->
<section id="s-formula" data-code="formula">
  <div class="section-no">02 — C = 6ND: The Core Formula</div>
  <h2>Everything Flows From One Equation</h2>
  <p>The single most important formula in LLM cost estimation is the <strong>compute budget</strong>:</p>

  <div class="math-block">
    <div class="math-label">Chinchilla compute formula</div>
    <span class="math-var">C</span> = 6 × <span class="math-var">N</span> × <span class="math-var">D</span>
    <br>
    <span class="math-comment">C = total training FLOPs, N = number of model parameters, D = training tokens</span>
    <br><br>
    <span class="math-comment">Each parameter needs ~2 FLOPs (forward) + ~4 FLOPs (backward) per token = 6 FLOPs total</span>
  </div>

  <p>Let's verify this on Llama 3 405B. Meta reported 3.8 × 10<sup>25</sup> FLOPs. Plugging in: 6 × 405B × 15.6T = 6 × 4.05 × 10<sup>11</sup> × 1.56 × 10<sup>13</sup> = <strong>3.79 × 10<sup>25</sup> FLOPs</strong>. Exact match.</p>

  <p>From FLOPs, you get GPU-hours via the <strong>effective throughput</strong> equation:</p>

  <div class="math-block">
    <div class="math-label">FLOPs to GPU-hours</div>
    <span class="math-var">GPU-hours</span> = <span class="math-var">C</span> / (<span class="math-var">P<sub>peak</sub></span> × <span class="math-var">MFU</span> × 3600)
    <br>
    <span class="math-comment">P_peak = GPU theoretical peak FLOPs/s (H100 SXM BF16 = 989 TFLOPS)</span>
    <br>
    <span class="math-comment">MFU = Model FLOP Utilization — how much of peak you actually use (Llama 3: 38–43%)</span>
  </div>

  <p>The interactive calculator below lets you adjust model size and training tokens to see how costs scale.</p>

  <div class="vp">
    <div class="vp-head">
      <span class="vp-title">Interactive Compute Budget Calculator</span>
      <span class="vp-hint">Click buttons to explore</span>
    </div>
    <div class="vp-body">
      <div class="calc-grid">
        <div class="calc-control">
          <div class="calc-label">Model size (N)</div>
          <div class="calc-btn-row" id="calc-n-btns">
            <button class="calc-btn" data-n="7">7B</button>
            <button class="calc-btn" data-n="70">70B</button>
            <button class="calc-btn active" data-n="405">405B</button>
            <button class="calc-btn" data-n="1000">1T</button>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="calc-control">
          <div class="calc-label">Training tokens (Chinchilla multiplier)</div>
          <div class="calc-btn-row" id="calc-tok-btns">
            <button class="calc-btn" data-tok="0.5">0.5× optimal</button>
            <button class="calc-btn" data-tok="1">1× optimal</button>
            <button class="calc-btn active" data-tok="2">2× (Llama 3)</button>
            <button class="calc-btn" data-tok="4">4× extended</button>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="calc-output" id="calc-output">
        <div class="calc-metric">
          <div class="calc-metric-val" id="calc-flops">—</div>
          <div class="calc-metric-label">Total FLOPs</div>
        </div>
        <div class="calc-metric">
          <div class="calc-metric-val" id="calc-gpu-hrs">—</div>
          <div class="calc-metric-label">H100 GPU-hours</div>
        </div>
        <div class="calc-metric">
          <div class="calc-metric-val" id="calc-cost">—</div>
          <div class="calc-metric-label">Compute cost @ $3/hr</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <canvas id="calc-canvas" style="width:100%;height:120px;border-radius:8px;margin-top:.5rem"></canvas>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3>The Chinchilla-Optimal Token Count</h3>
  <p>One key input is how many tokens to train on. The <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.15556" target="_blank" style="color:var(--cyan)">Chinchilla paper (Hoffmann et al., 2022)</a> showed the compute-optimal ratio is approximately <strong>20 tokens per parameter</strong>: for a 405B model, that's 405B × 20 = 8.1T tokens. Meta chose to train on 15.6T — nearly 2× Chinchilla-optimal. They explicitly stated that "smaller models are train for much longer than is compute-optimal" to improve quality at inference time. This is now the industry norm: train longer than optimal to get a better inference-time model.</p>
</section>

<!-- ── §03 Pre-Training ── -->
<section id="s-pretrain" data-code="pretrain">
  <div class="section-no">03 — Pre-Training: The 80% Cost</div>
  <h2>Pre-Training Dominates Everything</h2>
  <p>Pre-training is where you turn raw tokens into a foundation model. For Llama 3 405B, the numbers are:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>405B parameters</strong> in a standard dense Transformer (126 layers, dim 16,384, 128 heads)</li>
    <li><strong>15.6T training tokens</strong> — multilingual web text, code, math — processed through a 128K-vocab tokenizer</li>
    <li><strong>3.8 × 10<sup>25</sup> FLOPs</strong> total (from 6ND formula — verified against Meta's stated value)</li>
    <li><strong>16,384 H100 SXM 80GB GPUs</strong> at 700W TDP, connected via 400 Gbps InfiniBand/RoCE</li>
    <li><strong>MFU of 38–43%</strong> — that is, 38–43% of the GPU's theoretical peak BF16 throughput was doing actual model computation</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Translating FLOPs to GPU-Hours</h3>
  <p>The H100 SXM5 has a theoretical peak BF16 throughput of <strong>989 TFLOPS</strong> (without sparsity). At 40% MFU, the effective compute rate is 989 × 0.40 = 395.6 TFLOPS per GPU.</p>

  <div class="math-block">
    <div class="math-label">Llama 3 405B — GPU-hours calculation</div>
    GPU-seconds per GPU = 3.8 × 10<sup>25</sup> FLOPs / (3.956 × 10<sup>14</sup> FLOPs/s)
    <br>
    = 9.61 × 10<sup>10</sup> GPU-seconds
    <br>
    = <span class="math-var">26.7 million H100 GPU-hours</span>
    <br><br>
    <span class="math-comment">Cross-check: 16,384 GPUs × ~75 days × 24 h × 90% uptime ≈ 26.7M GPU-hours ✓</span>
  </div>

  <p>At a blended cloud reservation rate of $3.00/hr per H100 (see §05 for provider comparison), the pre-training compute cost is:</p>

  <div class="math-block">
    <div class="math-label">Pre-training compute cost</div>
    26.7M GPU-hours × $3.00/hr = <span class="math-var">~$80M</span>
    <br><br>
    <span class="math-comment">Range: $66M (Lambda/RunPod ~$2.50/hr reserved) to $107M (GCP/Azure on-demand ~$4/hr)</span>
  </div>

  <h3>The MFU Tax: Why Efficiency Matters</h3>
  <p>MFU is the single biggest lever you can pull on cost. Llama 3 achieved 40% MFU — excellent by industry standards, thanks to 4D parallelism (tensor, pipeline, context, and data parallelism). Many teams running their first large training run achieve 20–30% MFU, which doubles the cost. The visualization below shows the GPU-hours required at different MFU values.</p>

  <div class="vp">
    <div class="vp-head">
      <span class="vp-title">MFU vs. GPU-Hours Required (Llama 3 405B scale, 3.8 × 10²⁵ FLOPs)</span>
      <span class="vp-hint">Lower MFU = more GPU-hours = higher cost</span>
    </div>
    <div class="vp-body">
      <canvas id="mfu-canvas" style="width:100%;height:220px"></canvas>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3>Parallelism Configuration</h3>
  <p>Getting to 40%+ MFU on 16K GPUs requires careful parallelism setup. Llama 3 used 4D parallelism: 8-way tensor parallelism (TP=8, within a single node via NVLink), pipeline parallelism across nodes, context parallelism (CP=8 for the long-context stage), and data parallelism at the outer level. Getting this right is an engineering challenge of its own — budget 2–4 weeks of infrastructure iteration before your main training run.</p>
</section>

<!-- ── §04 Mid & Post-Training ── -->
<section id="s-pipeline" data-code="pipeline">
  <div class="section-no">04 — Mid-Training &amp; Post-Training</div>
  <h2>The 5% That Defines Product Quality</h2>
  <p>Once you have a pre-trained base model, you are only halfway done building a product. The full training pipeline for a frontier model looks like this:</p>

  <div class="step-list" id="pipeline-steps">
    <div class="step-box active">
      <div class="step-num">1</div>
      <div class="step-body">
        <div class="step-title">Pre-Training (initial)</div>
        <div class="step-desc">Train on 15.6T tokens with 8K context window. Uses 16K H100s for ~75 days. Batch size starts at 4M tokens, doubles twice to 16M.</div>
        <div class="step-code">C = 6 × 405B × 15.6T = 3.8e25 FLOPs → 26.7M GPU-hrs → ~$80M</div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="step-box">
      <div class="step-num">2</div>
      <div class="step-body">
        <div class="step-title">Annealing (pre-train finale)</div>
        <div class="step-desc">Final 40B tokens with learning rate annealed to 0. Upsample high-quality code and math data. Context window still 8K. Polyak-average checkpoints for the final base model.</div>
        <div class="step-code">~40B tokens × 405B params × 6 = 9.7e22 FLOPs → ~0.07M GPU-hrs → ~$200K</div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="step-box">
      <div class="step-num">3</div>
      <div class="step-body">
        <div class="step-title">Mid-Training: Long Context Extension</div>
        <div class="step-desc">Continue pre-training from the base checkpoint, extending context from 8K to 128K tokens. Requires special data with long-range dependencies. Typically 100B–500B tokens.</div>
        <div class="step-code">~300B tokens × 405B × 6 = 7.3e23 FLOPs → ~0.5M GPU-hrs → ~$1.5M</div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="step-box">
      <div class="step-num">4</div>
      <div class="step-body">
        <div class="step-title">Reward Model Training</div>
        <div class="step-desc">Fine-tune a copy of the model on human preference data (Bradley-Terry pairs). Typically 0.5M–5M preference examples. Relatively cheap since it's one SFT pass on a small dataset.</div>
        <div class="step-code">~5B tokens × 405B × 6 = 1.2e22 FLOPs → ~0.009M GPU-hrs → ~$27K</div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="step-box">
      <div class="step-num">5</div>
      <div class="step-body">
        <div class="step-title">Post-Training Round: SFT</div>
        <div class="step-desc">Supervised fine-tuning on high-quality instruction data (10M–50M tokens). Multiple rounds, each starting from the latest checkpoint. Llama 3 did several SFT rounds on ~10M curated examples per round.</div>
        <div class="step-code">Each SFT round: ~50B tokens × 405B × 6 = 1.2e23 FLOPs → ~0.08M GPU-hrs → ~$250K</div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="step-box">
      <div class="step-num">6</div>
      <div class="step-body">
        <div class="step-title">Post-Training Round: DPO</div>
        <div class="step-desc">Direct Preference Optimization — fine-tune on preference pairs without a separate reward model critic. Typically cheaper than RLHF. Llama 3 ran multiple DPO rounds iteratively improving quality.</div>
        <div class="step-code">Each DPO round: ~50B tokens × 405B × 6 = 1.2e23 FLOPs → ~0.08M GPU-hrs → ~$250K</div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="step-box">
      <div class="step-num">7</div>
      <div class="step-body">
        <div class="step-title">RLVR — Rollout Generation (2025+ reasoning models)</div>
        <div class="step-desc">Sample G=16–64 full completions per training prompt using the current policy. For reasoning models these are long chain-of-thought traces (avg 4K–32K tokens). Runs as autoregressive inference, which is memory-bandwidth limited — only 15–20% MFU. This is the single biggest cost sink in RLVR, often consuming 70–85% of wall-clock time per RL step.</div>
        <div class="step-code">100K steps × 32 prompts × 16 rollouts × 6K tokens = 307B rollout tokens → ~388K GPU-hrs @ 18% MFU → ~$1.2M</div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="step-box">
      <div class="step-num">8</div>
      <div class="step-body">
        <div class="step-title">RLVR — Policy Gradient + Reward Computation</div>
        <div class="step-desc">Score each rollout (code execution, symbolic math check, or a 70B reward model), then run GRPO/PPO policy gradient update. A frozen reference model copy also runs in parallel to compute the KL penalty. Policy gradient achieves normal training MFU (~40%) but reference-model inference adds another ~0.5× overhead.</div>
        <div class="step-code">Policy grad: 307B tok × 405B × 6 → 524K GPU-hrs → $1.6M | Ref model KL: 233K GPU-hrs → $0.7M | Reward/verifier: ~$0.3M</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-nav">
    <button id="pipe-prev" disabled>← Prev</button>
    <span id="pipe-prog" style="font-size:.72rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)">Step 1 of 8</span>
    <button id="pipe-next">Next →</button>
  </div>

  <h3>SFT/DPO Is Relatively Cheap — RLVR Is Not</h3>
  <p>For Llama 3-era models (SFT + DPO, 2023–2024), the total post-training compute was roughly <strong>1–2% of pre-training cost</strong>: about $1.5–3M for a 405B model. The big cost was not compute but <strong>data quality</strong> — curating 10M high-quality instruction examples and 500K preference pairs (see §06).</p>

  <p>But starting in 2025, frontier models replaced DPO with <strong>RLVR (Reinforcement Learning from Verifiable Rewards)</strong> — generating outcomes on-policy and scoring them with an automated verifier (code execution, symbolic math check). This fundamentally changes the cost structure: RLVR is inference-heavy rather than data-heavy, and those inference costs are substantial.</p>

  <h3>RLVR Cost Breakdown: Rollout, Reward, and Gradient</h3>
  <p>RLVR training cost splits into three distinct phases, each with a different compute profile. The numbers below use a reference configuration of 100K RL steps, batch = 32 prompts × G=16 rollouts = 512 rollouts/step, and average reasoning trace length of 6K tokens per rollout (typical for math/code reasoning).</p>

  <div class="vp">
    <div class="vp-head">
      <span class="vp-title">RLVR Post-Training — Compute Cost by Phase (405B model, 100K steps, G=16, L=6K)</span>
      <span class="vp-hint">All costs at $3/hr H100</span>
    </div>
    <div class="vp-body">
      <table class="results-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Phase</th><th>Mechanism</th><th>MFU</th><th>GPU-hours</th><th>Cost</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Rollout generation</strong></td>
            <td>Autoregressive inference: G completions of length L per prompt. Sequential token generation — memory-bandwidth limited.</td>
            <td class="mn">15–20%</td>
            <td class="mn">~388K</td>
            <td class="mn best">~$1.2M</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="highlight-row">
            <td><strong>Policy gradient update</strong></td>
            <td>Forward + backward pass on all G×L rollout tokens (GRPO/PPO loss). Runs like SFT — dense matrix ops, full MFU.</td>
            <td class="mn">38–42%</td>
            <td class="mn">~524K</td>
            <td class="mn best">~$1.6M</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Reference model (KL)</strong></td>
            <td>Forward pass on a frozen copy of the policy to compute the KL divergence regularization term.</td>
            <td class="mn">28–35%</td>
            <td class="mn">~233K</td>
            <td class="mn best">~$0.7M</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Reward / verifier</strong></td>
            <td>Code execution sandbox (CPU-heavy, modest GPU) + optional 70B reward model inference for open-ended tasks.</td>
            <td class="mn">—</td>
            <td class="mn">~80K</td>
            <td class="mn best">~$0.3M</td>
          </tr>
          <tr style="font-weight:700">
            <td>Total RLVR (100K steps)</td>
            <td></td>
            <td></td>
            <td class="mn best">~1.2M</td>
            <td class="mn best">~$3.8M</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p style="font-size:.72rem;color:var(--muted);margin-top:.75rem">⚠ Rollout generation is the dominant wall-clock cost even though it has fewer FLOPs than the gradient update — because autoregressive decoding runs at only 15–20% MFU vs 40% for training. Modern RLVR systems (verl, vLLM+Megatron hybrid, TBA-async) dedicate 70–90% of GPU capacity to inference at any given moment.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3>How Costs Scale with RL Training Depth</h3>
  <p>The numbers above are for 100K RL steps — a light-to-moderate run. Frontier reasoning models like o3 and Gemini 2.5 Pro almost certainly train much longer, and rollout lengths also increase as models learn to reason further. Here's how total RLVR cost scales:</p>

  <div class="results-bars" id="rlvr-scale-bars" style="margin:1.25rem 0">
    <div class="bar-row">
      <div class="bar-label">DeepSeek-R1-Zero scale (10K steps)</div>
      <div class="bar-track"><div class="bar-fill" style="background:rgba(239,68,68,.5)" data-w="5"><span>~$0.4M</span></div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="bar-row">
      <div class="bar-label">Research repro (50K steps)</div>
      <div class="bar-track"><div class="bar-fill" style="background:rgba(239,68,68,.65)" data-w="25"><span>~$2M</span></div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="bar-row">
      <div class="bar-label">Frontier light (100K steps)</div>
      <div class="bar-track"><div class="bar-fill" style="background:rgba(239,68,68,.75)" data-w="48"><span>~$3.8M</span></div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="bar-row">
      <div class="bar-label">Frontier mid (250K steps)</div>
      <div class="bar-track"><div class="bar-fill" style="background:rgba(239,68,68,.85)" data-w="75"><span>~$9.5M</span></div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="bar-row">
      <div class="bar-label">Frontier heavy (500K+ steps)</div>
      <div class="bar-track"><div class="bar-fill" style="background:#e06c75" data-w="100"><span>~$19M+</span></div></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>For comparison, traditional SFT+DPO for a 405B model costs approximately <strong>$2M</strong>. RLVR at frontier depth costs <strong>5–10× more</strong>. This is why post-training has moved from a minor line item to a first-class budget concern for 2025-era frontier labs — the reasoning capability it unlocks (AIME scores, competition math, code generation) is worth the compute, but it is decidedly not cheap.</p>

  <p>One important asymmetry: post-training failures are hard to fix with more compute. A pre-training run that's 5% worse than expected can often be rescued with better data or a longer run. A post-training run that produces a misaligned or low-quality assistant often requires going back to the drawing board on annotation quality. Budget for at least 3–4 post-training iteration cycles in both time and human annotation spend.</p>
</section>

<!-- ── §05 Cloud Comparison ── -->
