        <div class="bar-row">
          <div class="bar-label">CoreWeave (on-demand)</div>
          <div class="bar-track"><div class="bar-fill" style="background:var(--amber)" data-w="100"><span>$6.16/hr</span></div></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="font-size:.72rem;color:var(--muted);margin-top:.75rem">⚠ On-demand (spot/pay-as-you-go) prices shown. Large-cluster reservations (100+ nodes for 3–12 months) typically cost 30–50% less than on-demand. <br>For a training run at frontier scale, negotiate directly with CoreWeave, Lambda Labs, or your hyperscaler's AI team.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3>Which Provider for Training at Scale?</h3>
  <p>The choice of cloud provider for a frontier training run depends on three things beyond raw price:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Network fabric:</strong> H100 training at 16K+ GPU scale requires high-bandwidth inter-node interconnects (400 Gbps InfiniBand or RoCE). AWS p5 clusters use EFA at 3,200 Gbps per node. CoreWeave uses InfiniBand H100 fabric. GCP's A3 uses InfiniBand. Not all providers offer this for large clusters.</li>
    <li><strong>Reservation discounts:</strong> On-demand pricing is for small experiments. A 3–12 month reservation of 512–2048 GPUs typically earns 30–50% off on-demand. For a 26M GPU-hour run, negotiating from $3.73 to $2.50/hr saves ~$33M.</li>
    <li><strong>Support:</strong> Large training runs hit hardware failures daily. Llama 3 had 419 unexpected interruptions over 54 days. You need a cloud partner with 24/7 infrastructure support and fast node replacement SLAs.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>For the purposes of this cost model, we'll use <strong>$3.00/hr per H100</strong> as our blended assumption — achievable with a modest reservation at any major provider. Adjust up to $3.50–4.00 if you don't negotiate, or down to $2.00–2.50 if you negotiate a large multi-year commitment.</p>

  <h3>The A100 Alternative</h3>
  <p>A100 SXM 80GB nodes are available at $1.50–2.50/hr per GPU — roughly 40–60% cheaper than H100. The tradeoff: A100 BF16 peak throughput is 312 TFLOPS vs H100's 989 TFLOPS (3.2× slower), meaning you need 3.2× more GPU-hours. Net result: A100 training costs about 60–70% of H100 at the same reservation discount level. For a 405B-scale model, this is still a significant saving, but extends your wall-clock training time from 75 days to ~240 days — introducing more opportunity for hardware failures and iteration cycles. Most frontier labs prefer H100 for clock speed reasons.</p>
</section>

<!-- ── §06 Hidden Costs ── -->
<section id="s-hidden" data-code="hidden">
  <div class="section-no">06 — Hidden Costs</div>
  <h2>Compute Is 75%. The Other 25% Surprises Everyone</h2>
  <p>The "just compute" number is seductive because it's clean and measurable. But a functioning frontier lab requires significant non-compute spending that can add 25–35% to your total budget. Here is each category with rough estimates for a Llama 3 405B–scale project.</p>

  <h3>1. Data Infrastructure &amp; Storage</h3>
  <p>Llama 3 was trained on 15.6T tokens. At ~2 bytes per tokenized token (BF16 token IDs), the tokenized dataset is ~31TB. The raw un-processed web corpus is typically 5–10× larger: ~150–300TB. Plus you need to store model checkpoints throughout training.</p>

  <div class="results-table">
    <thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Size</th><th>Monthly Cost</th><th>Duration</th><th>Total</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Training data (tokenized)</td><td>~30 TB</td><td class="mn">~$600/mo</td><td>6 mo</td><td class="mn">~$3,600</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Raw web corpus archive</td><td>~200 TB</td><td class="mn">~$4,000/mo</td><td>6 mo</td><td class="mn">~$24,000</td></tr>
      <tr class="highlight-row"><td>Model checkpoints (405B at BF16)</td><td>810 GB × 1,000 ckpts = 810 TB</td><td class="mn">~$16,200/mo</td><td>4 mo</td><td class="mn best">~$65,000</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Checkpoint I/O throughput (burst)</td><td>—</td><td class="mn">~$2,000/mo</td><td>4 mo</td><td class="mn">~$8,000</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Intermediate activations/logs</td><td>~50 TB</td><td class="mn">~$1,000/mo</td><td>6 mo</td><td class="mn">~$6,000</td></tr>
      <tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>Total storage</strong></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td class="mn best"><strong>~$107,000</strong></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </div>

  <p>Storage is surprisingly cheap relative to compute — around $100K for the full run. The checkpointing cost dominates: a 405B model in BF16 is 810 GB per checkpoint. At 1,000 checkpoints (one every 1,200 training steps), that's 810TB of checkpoint data. Llama 3 used Meta's own distributed filesystem (Tectonic) which is presumably cheaper; on public cloud at $0.02/GB/month you'd pay ~$65K over 4 months.</p>

  <h3>2. Networking Egress</h3>
  <p>Public cloud providers charge for data transferred out of their network. For training, the dominant egress cost is loading your training dataset from object storage (S3/GCS) to the GPU cluster on every epoch. With 30TB of tokenized data read ~1× per epoch over multiple epochs, and egress at ~$0.08/GB, this is modest: 30TB × $0.08 = $2,400 per epoch. With 1–2 passes through the data, networking egress adds ~$5,000 total. Negligible.</p>
  <p>More relevant for a startup: <em>downloading</em> your model checkpoint for evaluation, deployment, or sharing. A single 810GB checkpoint download at $0.09/GB = $73. Not a budget item, but worth knowing.</p>

  <h3>3. Human Annotation (Post-Training Data)</h3>
  <p>This is the category most technical founders underestimate. The quality of a frontier chat model depends almost entirely on the quality of its post-training data — and getting that data requires human annotators.</p>

  <p>Llama 3 required several rounds of SFT and DPO data. A typical breakdown for a 405B model's post-training data program:</p>

  <div class="results-table">
    <thead><tr><th>Data Type</th><th>Scale</th><th>Cost/Example</th><th>Subtotal</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Initial SFT instructions (general)</td><td>~500K examples</td><td class="mn">$3–8</td><td class="mn">$1.5–4M</td></tr>
      <tr class="highlight-row"><td>Preference pairs (DPO/RLHF)</td><td>~200K pairs</td><td class="mn">$10–30</td><td class="mn best">$2–6M</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Safety / refusal data</td><td>~50K examples</td><td class="mn">$15–50</td><td class="mn">$0.75–2.5M</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Specialized capability data (code, math)</td><td>~200K</td><td class="mn">$5–20</td><td class="mn">$1–4M</td></tr>
      <tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>Total annotation budget</strong></td><td></td><td></td><td class="mn best"><strong>$5–17M</strong></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </div>

  <p>The wide range reflects the quality level you're targeting. Cheap annotation ($3–5/example) gives you a functional but mediocre instruction follower. Competitive frontier quality requires expert annotators with domain knowledge, especially for reasoning, coding, and safety — pushing costs to $15–50/example for those categories. For this estimate we assume a mid-range program totaling ~$4M.</p>

  <h3>4. Team and Engineering</h3>
  <p>This is the largest cost category that never appears in "training cost" headlines. Building and running a frontier model training effort requires:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Infrastructure engineers</strong> (5–10): build the training stack, handle cluster failures, optimize MFU, implement parallelism. These are rare and expensive.</li>
    <li><strong>Research scientists</strong> (5–15): design architecture, data pipeline, training recipe, evaluation suite.</li>
    <li><strong>ML engineers</strong> (5–10): data curation, post-training pipeline, evaluations.</li>
    <li><strong>Safety/policy</strong> (2–5): red-teaming, safety evaluations, responsible release planning.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Fully-loaded cost (salary + benefits + equity amortized + overhead) in the Bay Area: $350K–600K per person/year. A lean 20-person team over 18 months:</p>

  <div class="math-block">
    <div class="math-label">Team cost estimate</div>
    20 people × $450K/yr × 1.5 years = <span class="math-var">$13.5M</span>
    <br><br>
    <span class="math-comment">Add recruiting (30–40% first-year salary for key hires): +$1–2M</span>
    <br>
    <span class="math-comment">Office, compute for experiments, tooling, legal: +$1–2M</span>
    <br>
    <span class="math-comment">Total team + ops: ~$16–20M</span>
  </div>

  <p>Note that this 18-month period starts <em>before</em> the training run — you need 6–12 months of data pipeline work, scaling law experiments, and infrastructure setup before the big run begins. The team cost is a fixed overhead that runs in parallel with all the compute costs.</p>
</section>

