  <h3>Scaling to Other Model Sizes</h3>
  <p>The compute cost scales as <strong>N × D</strong>. If you're targeting a smaller model (say, 70B at 2× Chinchilla-optimal = 1.4T tokens), the pre-training compute is:</p>
  <div class="math-block">
    <div class="math-label">70B × 1.4T tokens</div>
    C = 6 × 70B × 1.4T = 5.88 × 10²³ FLOPs → 0.41M H100 GPU-hrs → <span class="math-var">~$1.2M</span>
    <br><br>
    <span class="math-comment">Total cost including team + ops: ~$20–30M for a competitive 70B model</span>
  </div>

  <p>A 7B model at 2× Chinchilla (140B tokens) costs ~$120K in compute alone — but that model is not frontier-class. Building something that's genuinely competitive today means 405B+ parameters or an architecture that punches above its weight (e.g., via mixture-of-experts, which can give more parameters-at-inference for the same FLOP count).</p>

