  <h3>Error Budget: Where the ±50% Comes From</h3>
  <p>The biggest sources of uncertainty in this estimate are:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>MFU (±20–40% on compute):</strong> A first-time team at 20% MFU instead of 40% MFU doubles the GPU-hour count. Conversely, a team with excellent infrastructure might reach 50%+ MFU, saving 20%.</li>
    <li><strong>GPU price negotiation (±30%):</strong> The difference between on-demand and a well-negotiated 12-month reservation is 30–50%.</li>
    <li><strong>Experiment overhead (±2×):</strong> A team that does more careful scaling law experiments before the big run can avoid costly restarts; a team that launches without scaling law validation may restart 1–2 times.</li>
    <li><strong>Data quality (1–2× post-training reruns):</strong> If post-training data quality is poor, you re-run post-training. Each rerun is small in compute cost ($1–3M) but significant in calendar time and annotation cost.</li>
    <li><strong>Team size (±50%):</strong> A 10-person tiger team vs a 40-person lab changes the team cost by $10–20M over 18 months.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>These factors combine roughly multiplicatively, which is why ±50% is a realistic error bar for a first estimate. The good news: the compute cost estimate based on 6ND is rock-solid (within ±10%) once you know N, D, and the GPU price.</p>
</section>

